  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is running ahead of plan year-to-date\u2014up 52% in revenue and 53% in appointments versus last year\u2014which is the one thing to be proud of here. Month-to-date, the store is tracking just 1.4% below DOW-adjusted expectation, so calendar mix accounts for the gap; yesterday's 15% dip is structural (Juneteenth in two days typically depresses bookings by ~10%), not operational. The real operational signal is add-on attach rate: it collapsed from 55% last Wednesday to 38% yesterday. That's a upsell discipline problem on the grooming floor. Core groom revenue fell 15%, but with only 5 active groomers producing $531 per groomer (above the 30-day baseline of $501), the team is running hot. The unit needs to recover upsell rigor before Juneteenth hits tomorrow.\n\nHicksville is outperforming expectations\u2014up 9.9% versus DOW-adjusted forecast and up 5% month-to-date\u2014but is signaling structural risk. Yesterday's add-on revenue was $30 against a typical $45 (33% miss), and critically, the 12+ week visit-gap cohort is growing (8.4% to 10.2%), indicating customers are stretching intervals and drifting quietly. With just 2 groomers scheduled through the weekend and zero bather hours, Hicksville is capacity-constrained and cannot service demand to retain that lapse pool. That's the thing to pay attention to. The 58-customer lapse pool ($39,960 annual value) is reachable, but only if you staff to answer the phone.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you have 7 active groomer-days across both stores with 7.0h bathers at Port Washington and 0h at Hicksville; tomorrow 8 groomer-days with the same bath split; next 7 days 39 scheduled groomer-days total, which should carry you through Juneteenth but leaves Hicksville thin."
